# Types of Sliding Window

There are two main types:

```text
1. Fixed-size sliding window
2. Variable-size sliding window
```

---

# 1. Fixed-Size Sliding Window

Use this pattern when the window size is already given.

### Common clues

```text
Subarray of size k
Substring of length k
Every window of size k
Permutation of a string
Maximum sum of k consecutive elements
```

---

## Fixed-Size Window Idea

Suppose the required window size is `k`.

1. Add the current right element.
2. If the window becomes larger than `k`, remove the left element.
3. When the window size is exactly `k`, calculate or check the answer.

---

## Fixed-Size Template

```java
int left = 0;

for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {

    // Add the right element to the window.
    add(input[right]);

    /*
     * If the window becomes larger than k,
     * remove the leftmost element.
     */
    if (right - left + 1 > k) {
        remove(input[left]);
        left++;
    }

    /*
     * The window now has exactly k elements.
     */
    if (right - left + 1 == k) {
        updateAnswer();
    }
}
```

---

## Example: Maximum Sum of Size `k`

```java
class Solution {
    public int maximumSum(int[] nums, int k) {

        int left = 0;
        int windowSum = 0;
        int maxSum = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for (int right = 0; right < nums.length; right++) {

            // Add the new element.
            windowSum += nums[right];

            // Keep the window size at most k.
            if (right - left + 1 > k) {
                windowSum -= nums[left];
                left++;
            }

            // Update the answer only for windows of size k.
            if (right - left + 1 == k) {
                maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, windowSum);
            }
        }

        return maxSum;
    }
}
```

---

## Fixed-Size Example

```text
nums = [2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2]
k = 3
```

Windows:

```text
[2, 1, 5] → sum = 8
[1, 5, 1] → sum = 7
[5, 1, 3] → sum = 9
[1, 3, 2] → sum = 6
```

Answer:

```text
9
```

