# Common Data Structures

The data structure depends on what information the window must store.

---

## 1. Running Sum

Use when the condition depends on the total sum.

```java
int windowSum = 0;

windowSum += nums[right];
windowSum -= nums[left];
```

Used in:

```text
LeetCode 209: Minimum Size Subarray Sum
```

---

## 2. HashSet

Use when the window must contain unique values.

```java
Set<Character> window = new HashSet<>();

window.add(character);
window.remove(character);
window.contains(character);
```

Used in:

```text
LeetCode 3: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters
```

---

## 3. HashMap

Use when frequencies or distinct values must be tracked.

```java
Map<Integer, Integer> frequency = new HashMap<>();

frequency.put(
    value,
    frequency.getOrDefault(value, 0) + 1
);
```

When removing:

```java
frequency.put(
    value,
    frequency.get(value) - 1
);

if (frequency.get(value) == 0) {
    frequency.remove(value);
}
```

Used in:

```text
LeetCode 76: Minimum Window Substring
LeetCode 904: Fruit Into Baskets
```

---

## 4. Frequency Array

Use when the character range is small and fixed.

For lowercase English letters:

```java
int[] frequency = new int[26];

frequency[character - 'a']++;
frequency[character - 'a']--;
```

For uppercase English letters:

```java
frequency[character - 'A']++;
```

Used in:

```text
LeetCode 424: Longest Repeating Character Replacement
LeetCode 567: Permutation in String
```

---

# How Character-to-Index Conversion Works

For lowercase letters:

```java
int index = character - 'a';
```

Mapping:

```text
'a' - 'a' = 0
'b' - 'a' = 1
'c' - 'a' = 2
...
'z' - 'a' = 25
```

For uppercase letters:

```java
int index = character - 'A';
```

This allows characters to be stored in an array of size `26`.

