# Common Mistakes

## Mistake 1: Forgetting `+1`

Wrong:

```java
int windowLength = right - left;
```

Correct:

```java
int windowLength = right - left + 1;
```

Both endpoints are included.

---

## Mistake 2: Moving `left` Without Removing Its Effect

Wrong:

```java
left++;
```

Correct:

```java
windowSum -= nums[left];
left++;
```

Or:

```java
frequency.put(
    nums[left],
    frequency.get(nums[left]) - 1
);

left++;
```

Remove the element’s effect before moving `left`.

---

## Mistake 3: Forgetting to Remove Zero-Frequency Keys

Wrong:

```java
frequency.put(value, frequency.get(value) - 1);
```

Correct:

```java
frequency.put(value, frequency.get(value) - 1);

if (frequency.get(value) == 0) {
    frequency.remove(value);
}
```

This matters when:

```java
frequency.size()
```

represents the number of distinct values.

---

## Mistake 4: Using `if` Instead of `while`

Wrong for variable-size windows:

```java
if (windowIsInvalid()) {
    left++;
}
```

Correct:

```java
while (windowIsInvalid()) {
    remove(input[left]);
    left++;
}
```

One removal may not be enough to restore validity.

---

## Mistake 5: Updating a Minimum Answer Outside the Shrinking Loop

For minimum-window problems, update while the window is valid:

```java
while (windowIsValid()) {
    minimumLength = Math.min(
        minimumLength,
        right - left + 1
    );

    remove(input[left]);
    left++;
}
```

This allows all smaller valid windows ending at `right` to be considered.

---

## Mistake 6: Updating Maximum Before Fixing the Window

Wrong:

```java
maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, right - left + 1);

while (windowIsInvalid()) {
    left++;
}
```

Correct:

```java
while (windowIsInvalid()) {
    remove(input[left]);
    left++;
}

maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, right - left + 1);
```

Never record an invalid window as the maximum.

---

## Mistake 7: Using Sliding Window When the Condition Is Not Monotonic

Sliding Window requires predictable behavior when the window expands or shrinks.

For example, LeetCode 209 contains only positive numbers:

```text
Adding an element increases the sum.
Removing an element decreases the sum.
```

If negative numbers are allowed, this behavior breaks.

For arrays containing negative numbers, techniques such as prefix sum, monotonic deque, or HashMap may be required instead.

